Ah, I just view it as a different experience. Angband is an almost pure
tactical combat exercise, Adom is a sort of adventure/rpg, and Nethack is a
1st edition AD&D session with an insane DM whacked out on magic mushrooms.
There are variants of *band that have fixed levels, static random dungeons,
overworld exploration, and a lot of other stuff. At its best, its a constant
struggle to optimize your equipment and inventory, and constantly deal with
dangerous situations that you get yourself into intelligently. At its worst,
its a scummy slog through reptitive random dungeons to acquire a single
missing intrinsic or stat gain potions... Some of the variants attempt to
address this though, and part of it is dictated by how you play the game
(intelligently set up macros, skillful use of all available resources, rapid
diving). My desire to play it comes and goes, just as with the others.

Dungeon Crawl is quite fun. I don't find it particularly deep, but that is
no bad thing. As you, I like the clean implementation of ac/ev,
spellcasting, and skills. Plus the insane variety of classes and races with
varying proficiencies lets you tackle the descent in a variety of ways. Come
to think of it, it reminds of Angband lite ;) Have you seen the japanese
developed tiled version? It works quite well for Crawl, due to the way the
display is set up, and it has spliced in some of Darshan's latest ui
improvements (notably instant travel to any explored point on the map,
autopickup of used missiles, and probably some other features I'm unaware
of) - http://crawlj.sourceforge.jp/down_e.html



